SST_RAW = [
    ("Why taxes on fuel raise so much",
     "Why do governments tax cigarettes, alcohol and fuel so heavily? Part of the answer lies in a concept economists call price elasticity of demand, which measures how much the quantity people buy changes when the price changes. For some goods, demand is elastic: if the price of one brand of cereal rises, many shoppers simply switch to another. For others, demand is inelastic, because people feel they need the product or have few alternatives. Fuel is a good example in the short term, since most drivers still need to get to work. Taxes on goods with inelastic demand raise large amounts of revenue, because sales fall only slightly. However, this also means such taxes may do less to change behaviour than their supporters hope. Over longer periods, demand often becomes more elastic, as people buy more efficient cars, move closer to work or, in the case of cigarettes, eventually quit.",
     "Price elasticity of demand describes how much purchases change when prices change. Goods with inelastic demand, such as fuel in the short term, keep selling when taxed, so taxes on them raise large revenue but may change behaviour less than expected. Over longer periods, demand usually becomes more elastic as people find alternatives, such as efficient cars or quitting smoking.",
     ["price elasticity", "inelastic", "revenue", "longer periods"]),
    ("Targeting inflation",
     "Many central banks today are given a clear goal: to keep inflation, the rate at which prices rise, close to a target, often around two percent a year. New Zealand was the first country to adopt such a target, in 1990, and many others followed. The main tool is the interest rate. When inflation is too high, the central bank raises rates, making borrowing more expensive, which tends to reduce spending and cool the economy. When inflation is too low, it cuts rates to encourage borrowing and spending. Why not aim for zero inflation? Economists argue that a small, stable rate gives some room to cut interest rates in a downturn and avoids the danger of falling prices, which can cause people to delay purchases. The approach works best when people trust that the bank will act, because expectations about future prices influence how businesses set prices and workers negotiate wages.",
     "Many central banks aim to keep inflation near a target, often about two percent, an approach New Zealand introduced in 1990. They raise interest rates to cool spending when inflation is high and cut them when it is low. A small positive target leaves room to cut rates and avoids falling prices, and success depends on trust shaping public expectations.",
     ["inflation", "target", "interest rates", "expectations"]),
    ("Who pays for tariffs",
     "A tariff is a tax on imported goods. Politicians sometimes describe tariffs as a way of making foreign countries pay, but economists point out that the reality is more complicated. The tax is paid at the border by the company importing the goods, and much of the cost is usually passed on to consumers through higher prices. Foreign producers may absorb part of it by lowering their prices to stay competitive, but studies of recent tariffs have often found that most of the burden fell on buyers in the country that imposed them. Tariffs can protect particular domestic industries from competition, preserving some jobs. However, they can also raise costs for other domestic businesses that rely on imported materials, and they may provoke other countries to respond with tariffs of their own, harming exporters. For these reasons, economists generally regard tariffs as a costly way of supporting industry.",
     "Tariffs are taxes on imports paid by importing companies, and much of the cost is usually passed on to domestic consumers rather than foreign producers. They can protect certain industries and jobs, but they raise costs for businesses using imported materials and may lead to retaliation that harms exporters, so economists generally see them as a costly form of support.",
     ["tariffs", "consumers", "protect", "retaliation"]),
    ("Public goods",
     "Economists use the term public good for something that has two special features. First, it is difficult to prevent anyone from benefiting from it. Second, one person's use does not reduce the amount available to others. Street lighting is a common example. Once a street is lit, everyone who walks along it benefits, and one extra person does not make the light dimmer. This creates a problem known as free riding. If a private company tried to sell street lighting, many residents would refuse to pay, knowing they would benefit anyway, so the service would probably not be provided at all, even though most people want it. This is why public goods such as street lighting, national defence and flood defences are usually funded through taxes. Economists debate which goods truly fit this definition, but the idea helps explain why some services are provided by governments rather than markets.",
     "A public good is one that nobody can easily be excluded from and whose use by one person does not reduce it for others, such as street lighting. Because people can benefit without paying, free riding means private firms would rarely supply it, so such goods, including defence and flood protection, are usually funded through taxes.",
     ["public good", "street lighting", "free riding", "taxes"]),
    ("Moral hazard",
     "Moral hazard is a term from the insurance industry. It describes the risk that people become less careful when they are protected from the consequences of their actions. Someone with full insurance on a bicycle, for example, might be less careful about locking it. Insurers respond in several ways. They often require customers to pay the first part of any claim themselves, known as an excess, so that the customer still has an incentive to avoid losses. They may also offer lower prices to people who take precautions, such as fitting alarms. The idea became widely discussed after the financial crisis of 2008, when governments rescued large banks. Critics argued that if banks expect to be saved when things go wrong, they may take bigger risks in the future. Regulators have since introduced rules requiring banks to hold more of their own capital, so that they carry more of the cost of their mistakes.",
     "Moral hazard is the tendency for people to take less care when protected from the consequences of their actions. In insurance, companies reduce it with an excess and discounts for precautions. After the 2008 crisis, bank rescues raised fears that banks would take bigger risks, so regulators now require banks to hold more capital and bear more of their losses.",
     ["moral hazard", "insurance", "excess", "capital"]),
    ("The market for lemons",
     "In 1970, the economist George Akerlof published a paper about the market for used cars, which helped him win a Nobel Prize. His key point was that sellers usually know much more about the quality of a car than buyers do. A buyer cannot easily tell a reliable car from a poor one, known in American slang as a lemon, so they are only willing to pay an average price. But at that price, owners of good cars may decide not to sell, because they would receive less than the car is worth. As good cars leave the market, the average quality falls, buyers lower the price they will pay, and the problem gets worse. This process is called adverse selection. Many features of modern markets exist partly to reduce it, including warranties, inspection reports, online reviews and brand reputations, all of which give buyers more reliable information.",
     "George Akerlof showed that in the market for used cars sellers know more about quality than buyers, so buyers pay only an average price. Owners of good cars then withdraw, lowering average quality and prices further, a process called adverse selection. Warranties, inspections, reviews and brand reputations help by giving buyers more reliable information about what they are buying.",
     ["used cars", "adverse selection", "quality", "warranties"]),
    ("Pigouvian taxes",
     "When a factory pollutes a river, the people living downstream bear part of the cost, yet the factory does not pay for it. Economists call such a cost an externality. Because the factory does not pay the full cost of its activities, it may produce more pollution than society would choose. In the early twentieth century, the British economist Arthur Pigou suggested a solution: tax the activity causing the harm, at a level matching the damage it does. The polluter then has a financial reason to reduce pollution, and it is free to decide how, whether by installing new equipment, changing its processes or producing less. Many economists favour such taxes because they reduce harm at the lowest overall cost. Setting the right level can be difficult, however, because the damage is not always easy to measure, and taxes can be politically unpopular, especially when they raise prices for consumers.",
     "An externality is a cost, such as pollution, that falls on others rather than on the business causing it, leading to too much harm. Arthur Pigou proposed a tax on such activities in line with the damage, giving polluters a reason to cut pollution in the cheapest way. However, damage is hard to measure and such taxes can be unpopular.",
     ["externality", "pollution", "tax", "unpopular"]),
    ("Automatic pension enrolment",
     "Many people know they should save for retirement, yet they put it off. Behavioural economists have found that the default option has a powerful effect on what people do. In traditional pension schemes, employees had to actively sign up, and many never did. When employers instead enrol workers automatically, while allowing them to leave the scheme if they wish, participation rises dramatically. The United Kingdom introduced automatic enrolment in workplace pensions from 2012, and the proportion of eligible employees saving into a pension increased substantially over the following years, particularly among young and lower-paid workers. Nobody is forced to save, because anyone can opt out, but inertia now works in favour of saving rather than against it. Critics point out that automatic enrolment does not guarantee that people save enough, and the default contribution rates may still leave many with modest retirement incomes.",
     "Because people often delay saving, the default option strongly affects pension participation. When the United Kingdom introduced automatic enrolment from 2012, allowing workers to opt out, the share of employees saving rose substantially, especially among young and lower-paid workers. However, critics note that default contribution rates may still leave many people with modest retirement incomes.",
     ["default", "automatic enrolment", "opt out", "retirement"]),
    ("Limits on rents",
     "Rent control, which limits how much landlords can charge or how quickly rents can rise, is popular with many tenants, who value the protection it offers against sudden increases. Most economists, however, have been sceptical of strict rent controls. They argue that when rents are held below market levels, landlords have less incentive to maintain their properties or to build new rental housing. Some owners may convert apartments into homes for sale, reducing the supply of rental housing. A well-known study of San Francisco found that tenants who benefited from rent control were more likely to stay in their homes, but landlords reduced the number of rental units available, which may have pushed rents up elsewhere in the city. Supporters of rent regulation respond that milder forms, such as limiting annual increases, can provide stability for tenants without the worst side effects, especially if combined with building more homes.",
     "Rent control protects tenants from sudden increases and is popular, but many economists argue that holding rents below market levels discourages maintenance and new building. A San Francisco study found tenants stayed longer but landlords reduced rental supply, possibly raising rents elsewhere. Supporters say milder limits, combined with building more homes, can give stability with fewer drawbacks.",
     ["rent control", "tenants", "supply", "San Francisco"]),
    ("M-Pesa",
     "In 2007, a mobile phone company in Kenya launched a service called M-Pesa, which allowed people to send and receive money using simple text messages. Customers could deposit and withdraw cash at small shops acting as agents. At the time, most Kenyans did not have a bank account, and sending money to relatives in rural areas often meant trusting a bus driver with an envelope of cash. M-Pesa spread rapidly, and within a few years it was used by a majority of adults in the country. Research suggested that access to the service helped households cope with unexpected problems, such as illness or a poor harvest, because relatives could quickly send support. One study estimated that it helped lift a significant number of households out of poverty. The service has since expanded to other countries, and it is often cited as an example of technology developed in Africa shaping financial services elsewhere.",
     "M-Pesa, launched in Kenya in 2007, let people send money by text message and use local shops to deposit and withdraw cash, serving many without a bank account. It spread rapidly, helping households cope with shocks such as illness or poor harvests through fast support from relatives, and research suggests it reduced poverty. It has since expanded to other countries.",
     ["M-Pesa", "bank account", "households", "poverty"]),
    ("The first public collections",
     "For centuries, collections of rare objects belonged to kings, nobles and wealthy scholars, and were usually seen only by invited guests. The idea of a museum open to the public developed gradually. One of the earliest examples is the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford, which opened in 1683 to house a collection donated to the university, and which admitted visitors who paid a fee. In the following century, the British Museum opened in London in 1759, allowing free entry, although visitors initially had to apply for tickets in advance. After the French Revolution, the Louvre in Paris opened in 1793, displaying former royal collections as the property of the nation. These institutions reflected new ideas that knowledge and culture should be shared more widely. Today museums continue to look for ways to reach people who rarely visit, through free entry, online collections and events.",
     "Collections of rare objects were once private, but public museums developed gradually. Early examples include the Ashmolean in Oxford in 1683, the British Museum in 1759 and the Louvre in 1793, which displayed royal collections as national property. They reflected the idea that culture should be shared, and museums still seek ways to reach people who rarely visit.",
     ["public", "Ashmolean", "Louvre", "shared"]),
    ("The founding of the Red Cross",
     "In 1859, a Swiss businessman named Henri Dunant witnessed the aftermath of the Battle of Solferino in northern Italy, where tens of thousands of soldiers lay wounded with little medical help. He organised local people to care for the injured, regardless of which side they had fought on. Afterwards he wrote a book describing what he had seen and proposing two ideas: that every country should have volunteer relief societies trained to help the wounded in war, and that there should be an international agreement protecting them. Both ideas became reality. The International Committee of the Red Cross was founded in Geneva in 1863, and in 1864 the first Geneva Convention was signed, protecting wounded soldiers and the medical staff caring for them. Dunant later shared the first Nobel Peace Prize in 1901, although by then he had lost his fortune and lived in poverty.",
     "After seeing thousands of wounded soldiers at Solferino in 1859, Henri Dunant organised care for both sides and proposed national volunteer relief societies and an international agreement protecting them. The Red Cross was founded in 1863 and the first Geneva Convention signed in 1864. Dunant shared the first Nobel Peace Prize in 1901, despite living in poverty.",
     ["Solferino", "Red Cross", "Geneva Convention", "Nobel Peace Prize"]),
    ("The first Paralympics",
     "The Paralympic Games grew out of work at Stoke Mandeville Hospital in England, where the neurologist Ludwig Guttmann treated soldiers and civilians with spinal injuries after the Second World War. At the time, such injuries were often seen as the end of an active life, but Guttmann believed that sport could aid physical rehabilitation and restore confidence. In 1948, on the same day the London Olympics opened, he organised an archery competition for patients in wheelchairs. The event grew each year and soon included international teams. In 1960, a competition held in Rome, just after the Olympic Games there, is now recognised as the first Paralympic Games, with around four hundred athletes. Over time, the Games expanded to include athletes with a wider range of impairments. Since 1988, the Paralympics have been held in the same city as the Olympics, and they now attract thousands of athletes and large global audiences.",
     "The Paralympics began with Ludwig Guttmann's work at Stoke Mandeville Hospital, where he used sport to rehabilitate people with spinal injuries after the Second World War. His 1948 wheelchair archery competition grew into international events, and the 1960 Rome games are recognised as the first Paralympics. The Games later included more impairments and, since 1988, share the Olympic host city.",
     ["Guttmann", "spinal injuries", "Rome", "1988"]),
    ("Where surnames come from",
     "For much of history, most people had only one name. As populations grew, this caused confusion, especially for officials collecting taxes or recording property. Surnames developed gradually to solve this problem, and in many European languages they fall into a few main types. Some describe a person's job, such as Smith, Baker or Miller. Others refer to where someone lived, such as Hill or Brook. Many come from a father's name, such as Johnson, meaning son of John, or its equivalents in other languages. A fourth group began as nicknames describing appearance or character. Not all cultures followed the same path. In Iceland, most people still use a name based on their father's or mother's first name rather than a fixed family surname. Japan made family names compulsory for all citizens only in 1875, when the government required everyone to register one.",
     "As populations grew, single names caused confusion for officials, so surnames developed gradually. In many European languages they come from jobs, places, a father's name or nicknames. Other cultures differ: people in Iceland still mostly use names based on a parent's first name, and Japan required all citizens to register a family name only in 1875.",
     ["surnames", "jobs", "father's name", "Iceland"]),
    ("A short history of the passport",
     "Travel documents have existed for centuries, but for most of history people could cross many borders without them. In the nineteenth century, the spread of railways made international travel much easier, and many European countries relaxed or abandoned passport requirements. The First World War changed this. Governments, worried about spies and keen to control the movement of people, introduced strict passport rules, and they remained in place after the war. In 1920, an international conference organised by the League of Nations agreed a standard format, a small booklet with a photograph and a description of the holder, which was the basis for the modern passport. Many travellers at the time objected to having their photographs taken for official purposes, seeing it as treating them like criminals. Since then, passports have added machine-readable pages and electronic chips storing the holder's details.",
     "A passport was often unnecessary in the nineteenth century, when railways made travel easier and rules were relaxed. The First World War brought strict controls that remained afterwards, and in 1920 a League of Nations conference agreed a standard booklet with a photograph, the basis of today's passport. Some travellers objected, and modern passports add electronic chips.",
     ["passport", "First World War", "League of Nations", "photograph"]),
    ("The oldest universities",
     "Which is the oldest university in the world? The answer depends on how a university is defined. The University of al-Qarawiyyin in Fez, Morocco, founded in 859 by a woman named Fatima al-Fihri, is often described as the oldest continuously operating institution of higher learning, although it began as a mosque with a school attached. In Europe, the University of Bologna, dating from 1088, is usually considered the first university in the modern sense: a self-governing community of students and teachers that awarded degrees. Interestingly, at Bologna it was the students who initially held much of the power, hiring teachers and even fining them if they started late or skipped difficult topics. Oxford and Paris followed soon after, with teachers playing a larger role in their organisation. These medieval institutions established features that still exist today, including degrees, faculties and academic dress.",
     "The answer depends on definition. Al-Qarawiyyin in Morocco, founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri, is often called the oldest continuously operating institution of higher learning, while Bologna, from 1088, is usually seen as the first university in the modern sense. At Bologna, students initially held power over teachers, and medieval universities established degrees, faculties and academic dress.",
     ["al-Qarawiyyin", "Bologna", "students", "degrees"]),
    ("A slow welcome for the fork",
     "Forks now seem an essential part of eating in many countries, but they were slow to be accepted in Europe. Large forks were used in kitchens for centuries, but small forks for eating were more common in the Byzantine Empire. According to a famous story, when a Byzantine princess married into a Venetian noble family in the eleventh century and ate with a golden fork, some church leaders condemned her behaviour as excessively refined. Forks gradually spread through Italy, where they were useful for eating pasta, but many other Europeans continued to eat with knives, spoons and their fingers. In the early seventeenth century, an English traveller described Italian forks as a curious custom. It was not until the eighteenth century that forks became common in much of Europe. In many parts of the world, of course, people continued to eat with chopsticks or their hands, which remain the most common methods globally.",
     "Eating forks were used in the Byzantine Empire but spread slowly in Europe. A Byzantine princess using a golden fork in Venice was reportedly criticised, and forks became popular in Italy partly for pasta, while elsewhere people used knives, spoons and fingers until the eighteenth century. Worldwide, chopsticks and hands remain the most common ways of eating.",
     ["forks", "Byzantine", "Italy", "chopsticks"]),
    ("The history of the toothbrush",
     "People have cleaned their teeth for thousands of years, using chewed twigs from certain trees, some of which are still used today in parts of Africa, the Middle East and South Asia. The bristle toothbrush appears to have been developed in China, where records describe brushes made with hairs from pigs attached to handles of bone or bamboo. In England, a man named William Addis is credited with starting the first mass production of toothbrushes in the late eighteenth century. According to the company's history, he had the idea while in prison, when he made a brush from a small bone and some bristles. Brushing did not become a daily habit for most people until the twentieth century. Soldiers in the Second World War were required to brush their teeth and brought the habit home. The introduction of nylon bristles in the 1930s also made toothbrushes cheaper and more hygienic.",
     "People cleaned their teeth with chewed twigs for thousands of years, and bristle toothbrushes were developed in China using pig hair. William Addis began mass production in England in the eighteenth century, reportedly after making a brush in prison. Daily brushing became common only in the twentieth century, helped by wartime habits and cheaper, more hygienic nylon bristles.",
     ["twigs", "China", "William Addis", "nylon"]),
    ("Loneliness and health",
     "Loneliness is usually thought of as an emotional problem, but researchers increasingly treat it as a public health issue. Large studies following people over many years have found that social isolation and loneliness are associated with a higher risk of early death, as well as heart disease, stroke and depression. One widely discussed analysis suggested that the effect on mortality was comparable to well-known risk factors such as obesity. Scientists are still investigating why. Loneliness may cause chronic stress, disrupt sleep and make people less likely to exercise or seek medical help. Importantly, being alone and feeling lonely are not the same; some people live alone happily, while others feel lonely in a crowd. In 2018, the United Kingdom appointed a government minister with responsibility for loneliness, and some doctors now refer patients to community groups and activities, an approach known as social prescribing.",
     "Researchers increasingly treat loneliness as a public health issue, since studies link isolation and loneliness to early death, heart disease, stroke and depression, possibly through stress, poor sleep and less healthy behaviour. Being alone differs from feeling lonely. The United Kingdom appointed a minister for loneliness in 2018, and doctors may use social prescribing to connect patients with community activities.",
     ["loneliness", "public health", "early death", "social prescribing"]),
    ("Exercise and low mood",
     "Physical activity is well known to benefit the heart and muscles, but there is also strong evidence that it helps mental health. Reviews combining many studies have found that regular exercise can reduce symptoms of depression, and in some trials its effect was comparable to that of talking therapies or medication for mild to moderate cases. Several explanations have been proposed. Exercise may affect chemicals in the brain linked to mood, improve sleep, reduce inflammation and provide a sense of achievement. Group activities can also offer social contact. Importantly, benefits have been found for many types of exercise, including walking, and not only for intense training. However, experts stress that exercise is not a replacement for professional treatment in severe depression, and that one of the difficulties is that depression itself reduces energy and motivation, so people may need support to get started.",
     "Research shows that regular exercise can reduce symptoms of depression, sometimes as much as therapy or medication in mild to moderate cases, possibly by affecting brain chemicals, sleep, inflammation and confidence. Many activities, including walking, help. However, exercise does not replace treatment for severe depression, and because depression reduces motivation, people may need support to start.",
     ["exercise", "depression", "walking", "motivation"]),
    ("Short sight and outdoor play",
     "Short-sightedness, or myopia, has become far more common in recent decades. In some cities in East Asia, a large majority of young people finishing school are now short-sighted, compared with a much smaller share a few generations ago. The change has happened too quickly to be explained by genes alone. For many years, reading and close work were blamed, but research increasingly points to another factor: time spent outdoors. Children who spend more time outside tend to be less likely to become short-sighted, and one explanation is that bright daylight affects how the eye grows. In a trial in China, schools that added an extra outdoor class each day saw fewer new cases of myopia. As a result, some countries now encourage schools to give children more time outside. Severe myopia matters because it increases the risk of serious eye diseases later in life.",
     "Myopia has risen sharply, especially in East Asian cities, too quickly for genes alone to explain. Research suggests a lack of time outdoors is important, possibly because bright daylight affects eye growth, and a Chinese trial found an extra daily outdoor class reduced new cases. Some countries now encourage outdoor time, since severe myopia raises the risk of eye disease.",
     ["myopia", "outdoor", "daylight", "eye disease"]),
    ("How painkillers work",
     "Common painkillers work in different ways. Drugs such as ibuprofen and aspirin belong to a group called non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. They block enzymes that produce substances called prostaglandins, which contribute to pain, fever and swelling. Because they reduce inflammation, they are often recommended for injuries such as sprains. However, they can irritate the stomach and are not suitable for everyone. Paracetamol, known as acetaminophen in some countries, also reduces pain and fever, but it has little effect on inflammation, and scientists are still not entirely sure how it works. It is gentle on the stomach, but taking more than the recommended dose can seriously damage the liver, which is why pack sizes are limited in some countries. Doctors often point out that combining different painkillers safely can be more effective than increasing the dose of one, but people should always follow medical advice.",
     "Anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen and aspirin block prostaglandins, reducing pain, fever and swelling, but they can irritate the stomach. Paracetamol reduces pain and fever with little effect on inflammation, and how it works is not fully understood; it is gentle on the stomach, but overdoses can damage the liver. Combining painkillers safely may help, following medical advice.",
     ["ibuprofen", "inflammation", "paracetamol", "liver"]),
    ("Lactase persistence",
     "All human babies can digest lactose, the sugar in milk, using an enzyme called lactase. In most mammals, including most humans historically, production of this enzyme falls after weaning, so adults who drink large amounts of milk may suffer from stomach discomfort. However, in some populations, many adults continue producing lactase throughout life. This trait, called lactase persistence, is common in northern Europe and among some herding groups in East Africa and the Middle East. Genetic studies show that it evolved separately in different regions, through different mutations, within the last several thousand years, after people began keeping dairy animals. This is one of the clearest examples of recent human evolution, and of culture shaping biology: where milk was an important food, people who could digest it may have had an advantage. Worldwide, however, most adults still have a reduced ability to digest lactose.",
     "Babies digest lactose, but in most people lactase production falls after weaning. In some populations, including northern Europeans and certain East African and Middle Eastern herders, lactase persistence evolved separately within the last few thousand years after dairy farming began. It is a clear example of recent evolution shaped by culture, though most adults worldwide still digest milk poorly.",
     ["lactase", "milk", "evolved", "dairy"]),
    ("Why fingers wrinkle",
     "After a long bath, our fingers and toes become wrinkled. For a long time, this was thought to be a simple result of skin absorbing water and swelling. But in the 1930s, doctors noticed that fingers did not wrinkle if the nerves to them had been damaged, showing that wrinkling is an active process controlled by the nervous system. When skin is soaked, blood vessels beneath it narrow, and the skin surface folds. Why would the body do this? In 2013, researchers reported that people with wrinkled fingers were quicker at moving wet objects from one container to another, suggesting that wrinkles might improve grip in wet conditions, like the tread on a tyre. Other studies, however, have not found the same advantage, so the question remains open. Doctors have occasionally used the wrinkling test to check whether nerves in the hand are working properly.",
     "Fingers wrinkle in water not through simple swelling but through an active process controlled by nerves, since damaged nerves prevent it; blood vessels narrow and the skin folds. A 2013 study suggested wrinkles improve grip on wet objects, but other studies disagree, so the purpose remains uncertain. Doctors have used the test to check nerves in the hand.",
     ["wrinkle", "nerves", "grip", "uncertain"]),
    ("Learning styles",
     "Many people believe that each of us has a preferred learning style, such as visual, auditory or hands-on, and that students learn best when teaching matches that style. Surveys have found that large numbers of teachers around the world accept this idea. Yet when researchers have tested it properly, the evidence has been weak. To support the theory, a study would need to show that visual learners do better with visual teaching while auditory learners do better with spoken teaching. Reviews of the research have found very few studies meeting this standard, and those that exist generally do not support the idea. People certainly have preferences, but preferring a method does not mean learning more from it. Experts suggest that the best approach often depends on the content: maps are best understood visually, for example, and pronunciation through listening. Using several methods together can help everyone.",
     "Many teachers believe students learn best when teaching matches their preferred learning style, such as visual or auditory. However, properly designed studies provide little evidence for this, and preferences do not mean better learning. Experts argue that the best method depends on the content, such as maps shown visually or pronunciation taught through listening, and combining methods helps all students.",
     ["learning style", "evidence", "preferences", "content"]),
    ("Teachers' expectations",
     "In the 1960s, the psychologists Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson told teachers at an American school that certain pupils had been identified by a test as likely to make rapid intellectual progress. In reality, these pupils had been chosen at random. At the end of the year, some of the selected children, particularly younger ones, showed greater gains in test scores than their classmates. The researchers concluded that teachers' expectations had influenced the pupils' progress, perhaps because teachers gave them more attention, encouragement and challenging work. The study became famous as the Pygmalion effect. It has also been criticised. Later researchers questioned its methods, and attempts to repeat it found smaller or inconsistent effects. Most psychologists now agree that teacher expectations can influence pupils, but that the effect is usually modest rather than dramatic. Even so, the idea encourages teachers to hold high expectations for all students.",
     "In the 1960s, teachers were told that randomly chosen pupils would progress rapidly, and some of those pupils, especially younger ones, later gained more. Researchers concluded that expectations shaped progress through attention and encouragement, the Pygmalion effect. Later studies questioned the methods and found smaller effects, so the influence is considered real but modest, encouraging teachers to hold high expectations for everyone.",
     ["expectations", "Pygmalion effect", "modest", "teachers"]),
    ("Testing yourself to remember",
     "Many students revise by rereading their notes and textbooks, often several times. It feels productive, because the material becomes familiar. However, research on memory suggests that a different method is much more effective: testing yourself. In many experiments, students who practised recalling information, for example by answering questions or writing down everything they could remember, performed better on later tests than students who spent the same time rereading. This is known as the testing effect, or retrieval practice. The effort of retrieving information from memory seems to strengthen it and make it easier to recall in the future. Tests also reveal what a student does not know, allowing them to focus on weak areas. Researchers note that the practice tests do not need to be marked or to count towards grades. Simple techniques, such as flashcards or covering notes and explaining a topic aloud, can provide the same benefit.",
     "Rereading notes feels productive because material becomes familiar, but research shows that testing yourself is more effective. In experiments, students who practised recalling information outperformed those who spent the time rereading, because retrieval strengthens memory and reveals gaps in knowledge. Practice tests need not be marked, and simple methods such as flashcards or explaining topics aloud provide the same benefit.",
     ["rereading", "testing", "retrieval", "flashcards"]),
    ("Note-taking methods",
     "Do students learn more by taking notes by hand or on a laptop? A widely reported study in 2014 found that students who typed tended to copy lectures word for word, while those who wrote by hand, being slower, had to summarise in their own words. On questions testing understanding, the handwriting group performed better. The study was influential, and some lecturers banned laptops from their classrooms. However, later attempts to repeat the experiment produced mixed results, and a larger replication found that the difference was small and not reliable. What seems more consistent is that the way students process information matters more than the tool. Summarising, reorganising ideas and reviewing notes later all help learning, whichever method is used. Laptops also bring a separate problem: distraction. Studies have found that students who browse the internet during lectures learn less, and may even distract classmates sitting nearby.",
     "A 2014 study suggested that handwriting improves understanding because slower writers summarise rather than copy word for word, and some lecturers banned the laptop from class. However, a larger replication found small, unreliable differences. How students process information, by summarising and reviewing notes, matters more than the tool, although laptops can cause distraction for users and nearby classmates.",
     ["handwriting", "laptop", "replication", "distraction"]),
    ("Shared picture-book reading",
     "Reading aloud to young children is often recommended by teachers and health professionals, and research supports the advice. Picture books contain a wider range of vocabulary than most everyday conversation, so children who are read to regularly hear many words they would rarely encounter otherwise. One study estimated that children whose parents read to them daily would hear over a million more words by the time they started school than children who were never read to. Shared reading also helps children understand how stories are structured, connect spoken and written language, and develop concentration. Benefits appear to be greatest when adults make it interactive, asking questions, pointing to pictures and encouraging children to predict what will happen next, rather than simply reading the words. Importantly, the language does not need to be the language of school; reading in a family's home language supports development just as well.",
     "Research supports reading aloud to young children, because picture books contain richer vocabulary than everyday conversation, and daily reading may expose children to over a million extra words before school. It also builds understanding of stories and concentration. Benefits are greatest when reading is interactive, with questions and predictions, and reading in the home language works equally well.",
     ["reading aloud", "vocabulary", "interactive", "home language"]),
    ("Smaller classes",
     "Does reducing class sizes improve learning? One of the most influential studies, known as Project STAR, took place in the American state of Tennessee in the late 1980s. Thousands of young pupils and their teachers were randomly assigned to small classes of around fifteen pupils or to regular classes of around twenty-two to twenty-five. Because the assignment was random, differences in results could be linked to class size itself. Pupils in small classes performed better in the early years, and the benefits were larger for children from disadvantaged backgrounds. Some follow-up studies found lasting advantages, such as higher rates of college entry. However, smaller classes are expensive, because they require more teachers and classrooms. When California reduced class sizes rapidly in the 1990s, it had to hire many inexperienced teachers, and the gains were smaller than expected. Policymakers therefore weigh class size against other uses of the same money.",
     "Tennessee's Project STAR randomly assigned pupils to small or regular classes and found that a smaller class size improved early results, especially for disadvantaged children, with some lasting benefits. However, smaller classes are costly, and California's rapid reduction required many inexperienced teachers and produced smaller gains, so policymakers compare class size with other uses of money.",
     ["class size", "randomly", "disadvantaged", "teachers"]),
    ("Unequal internet access",
     "The term digital divide describes the gap between people who have access to the internet and digital technology and those who do not. Globally, a large share of the population still does not use the internet, and most of these people live in low-income countries and rural areas. But the divide is not only about access. Researchers also point to differences in the quality of connection, the devices people use and the skills needed to benefit from them. A student trying to complete homework on a shared mobile phone with limited data faces very different conditions from one with a laptop and fast broadband. These differences became especially visible when schools closed during the pandemic and lessons moved online. Efforts to close the divide include expanding mobile networks, reducing data costs, providing devices through schools and libraries, and teaching digital skills to older adults and others who lack confidence online.",
     "The digital divide is the gap between those with and without internet access, especially in low-income and rural areas. It also includes connection quality, devices and digital skills, differences exposed when schools moved online during the pandemic. Solutions include expanding networks, lowering data costs, providing devices through schools and libraries, and teaching skills to those who lack confidence.",
     ["digital divide", "access", "skills", "pandemic"]),
    ("Ranking web pages",
     "In the early days of the web, search engines ranked pages mainly by counting how often the search words appeared on them. This was easy to manipulate: a page could simply repeat popular words many times. In the late 1990s, two graduate students at Stanford University, Larry Page and Sergey Brin, developed a different approach called PageRank. It treated a link from one page to another as a kind of vote. A page linked to by many other pages was likely to be important, and a link from an important page counted for more than a link from an obscure one. This idea helped their search engine, Google, produce much more useful results, and it grew rapidly. Modern search engines use hundreds of signals, and link-based ranking led to new forms of manipulation, such as creating networks of fake links, which search companies continue to fight.",
     "Early search engines ranked pages by counting search words, which was easily manipulated. Larry Page and Sergey Brin developed PageRank, treating links as votes and giving links from important pages more weight, which helped Google produce better results. Modern search engines use hundreds of signals, and link-based ranking created new manipulation, such as fake link networks, that companies still fight.",
     ["search engines", "PageRank", "links", "manipulation"]),
    ("Moore's law",
     "In 1965, Gordon Moore, who later co-founded the chip maker Intel, observed that the number of components that could be placed on a computer chip was doubling roughly every year. He later revised the rate to about every two years. His observation, known as Moore's law, was not a law of nature but a prediction, and for decades the industry treated it almost as a target. As transistors became smaller, chips became faster, cheaper and more energy-efficient, which explains why a modern smartphone is far more powerful than the computers that guided the first Moon landings. In recent years, however, progress has slowed. Transistors are now so small that they approach the size of atoms, making further shrinking extremely difficult and expensive. Engineers are responding in other ways, such as stacking components in three dimensions and designing specialised chips for particular tasks, such as artificial intelligence.",
     "In 1965 Gordon Moore observed that chip components were doubling regularly, later about every two years. Moore's law was a prediction the industry treated as a target, and shrinking transistors made chips faster, cheaper and more efficient. Progress has slowed as transistors approach atomic size, so engineers now stack components and design specialised chips for tasks such as artificial intelligence.",
     ["Moore's law", "transistors", "slowed", "specialised chips"]),
    ("The millennium bug",
     "In the late 1990s, governments and businesses around the world became concerned about a problem known as the millennium bug, or Y2K. Many older computer systems stored years using only their last two digits, to save expensive memory. The year 1999 was recorded as ninety-nine, so when the date changed to 2000, some systems might read it as 1900, causing errors in calculations involving dates, from bank interest to scheduling. Organisations spent enormous sums checking and correcting their software. When the new year arrived, very few serious problems occurred. Some people concluded that the danger had been exaggerated and the money wasted. Many engineers who worked on the problem argue the opposite: that failures were rare precisely because so much work had been done in advance. The episode is sometimes used to illustrate a difficulty with prevention. When a problem is successfully avoided, it can look as though it never existed.",
     "The millennium bug arose because older systems stored years with two digits, so 2000 might be read as 1900, causing date errors. Organisations spent heavily fixing software, and few problems occurred. Some called the fears exaggerated, but engineers argue the preparation prevented failures, illustrating how successful prevention can make a problem look as though it never existed.",
     ["millennium bug", "two digits", "prevention", "software"]),
    ("Packet switching",
     "When you send a photo over the internet, it does not travel as a single piece. Instead, it is broken into many small packets of data, each labelled with its destination and its place in the sequence. The packets may travel by different routes through the network, and at the other end they are put back together in the right order. If a packet is lost or damaged, it can simply be sent again. This method, called packet switching, was developed in the 1960s by researchers in the United States and Britain. It differed from the telephone network, which created a dedicated connection for the whole length of a call. Packet switching makes much more efficient use of the network, because many users can share the same connections, and it is resilient, since data can be routed around damaged or congested parts of the network. It remains a foundation of the internet today.",
     "Internet data is split into labelled packets that may travel by different routes and are reassembled at the destination, with lost packets sent again. Packet switching, developed in the 1960s in the United States and Britain, differs from dedicated telephone connections. It is efficient because users share connections, and resilient because data can avoid damaged or congested routes.",
     ["packets", "packet switching", "efficient", "resilient"]),
    ("Range anxiety",
     "When people consider buying an electric car, one of their most common concerns is range anxiety: the fear that the battery will run out before they reach a charging point. Surveys suggest this worry often exceeds the reality. Most daily journeys are far shorter than the range of modern electric cars, and many owners charge at home overnight, so they start each day with a full battery. Long journeys still require planning, and the availability and reliability of public chargers vary greatly between regions. Cold weather can also reduce range noticeably, because batteries work less efficiently and energy is needed to heat the car. Researchers have found that range anxiety tends to fall after people gain experience with electric cars, as they learn how far they can actually travel. Governments and companies are investing in faster charging networks, particularly along motorways, to address the concerns of people who have not yet switched.",
     "Range anxiety, the fear that an electric car's battery will run out, is common but often exceeds reality, since most journeys are short and many owners charge at home. Long trips need planning, public chargers vary, and cold weather reduces range. The worry usually falls with experience, and investment in faster charging networks aims to reassure new buyers.",
     ["range anxiety", "charge at home", "cold weather", "experience"]),
    ("QR codes",
     "QR codes, the square black-and-white patterns scanned with phone cameras, were invented in 1994 by an engineer at a Japanese company that made parts for the car industry. They were designed to track components in factories, and could store far more information than traditional barcodes, which can only be read in one direction. The name stands for quick response. The codes can be read from any angle, and they include extra information that allows them to be scanned even if part of the pattern is damaged or dirty. The company chose not to enforce its patent, allowing anyone to use the design freely, which helped it spread. For many years, QR codes were popular in parts of Asia but less so elsewhere, partly because phones needed separate apps to read them. When phone cameras began reading them automatically, and the pandemic encouraged contactless menus and payments, they became common worldwide.",
     "QR codes were invented in Japan in 1994 to track car parts and store more information than barcodes. They can be read from any angle and even when damaged, and the company allowed free use of the design. Once phone cameras could read them automatically and the pandemic encouraged contactless menus and payments, they spread worldwide.",
     ["QR codes", "barcodes", "damaged", "pandemic"]),
    ("The mantis shrimp's punch",
     "The mantis shrimp is a small, brightly coloured marine animal with one of the most powerful strikes in the animal kingdom. Some species have club-like limbs that they fire forward at extraordinary speed to smash the shells of crabs and snails. The strike is so fast that it causes bubbles to form and collapse in the water, producing an additional shock wave, a process called cavitation. In aquariums, mantis shrimp have been known to crack glass. Scientists have studied how the animal's clubs survive repeated impacts without breaking. They are built in layers arranged in a spiral pattern, which stops cracks from spreading. This structure has inspired engineers developing lightweight, impact-resistant materials for helmets, body armour and aircraft. Mantis shrimp are also famous for their eyes, which contain many more types of colour receptors than human eyes, although experiments suggest they are not better than us at telling similar colours apart.",
     "The mantis shrimp strikes prey with club-like limbs so fast that collapsing bubbles create an extra shock wave, and it can even crack aquarium glass. Its clubs resist damage thanks to spiral layers that stop cracks spreading, inspiring impact-resistant materials. Its eyes have many colour receptors, though experiments suggest it does not distinguish similar colours better than humans.",
     ["mantis shrimp", "shock wave", "layers", "colour receptors"]),
    ("Cuckoos in the nest",
     "The common cuckoo does not build a nest or raise its own young. Instead, the female lays her eggs in the nests of other bird species, a strategy known as brood parasitism. Her egg often closely resembles those of the host species in colour and pattern, reducing the chance that it will be rejected. After hatching, the cuckoo chick usually pushes the host's eggs or chicks out of the nest, so that it receives all the food. The host parents then raise the cuckoo, even when it grows much larger than they are. This relationship has been described as an evolutionary arms race. Host species that learn to recognise and reject strange eggs survive better, so cuckoos that lay more convincing eggs are favoured, and so on. Some host populations have become very good at detecting cuckoo eggs, while cuckoos in turn have evolved to lay their eggs very quickly, sometimes in only a few seconds.",
     "Cuckoos lay their eggs in other birds' nests, a strategy called brood parasitism, and the eggs often resemble those of the host. The cuckoo chick pushes out the host's eggs or chicks and is raised by the hosts. This creates an evolutionary arms race, with hosts learning to reject strange eggs and cuckoos evolving more convincing eggs and very quick laying.",
     ["brood parasitism", "host", "arms race", "eggs"]),
    ("Dragonflies on the hunt",
     "Dragonflies are among the most successful hunters in the animal world. Studies have found that some species catch their prey in the vast majority of attempts, a success rate far higher than that of lions or sharks. Their secret lies partly in their flight. Each of their four wings can be controlled independently, allowing them to hover, change direction instantly and even fly backwards. But researchers believe their brains matter just as much. Rather than simply chasing their prey, dragonflies predict where it will go and fly on a course to intercept it, much as a fielder runs to where a ball will land. Specialised nerve cells help them track a single target while ignoring others in a swarm. Dragonflies have existed for hundreds of millions of years, and some ancient relatives had wingspans of around seventy centimetres. Engineers study their flight and targeting to design small drones.",
     "Dragonflies are extremely successful hunters, catching prey in most attempts. Independently controlled wings let them hover, turn instantly and fly backwards, and they predict where prey will go and intercept it, using specialised nerve cells to track one target in a swarm. They have ancient origins, and engineers study their flight and targeting to design small drones.",
     ["dragonflies", "wings", "intercept", "drones"]),
    ("Do dogs feel guilty?",
     "Many dog owners are convinced that their dogs look guilty when they have done something wrong, such as eating food from the table. In 2009, the researcher Alexandra Horowitz tested this belief. Owners were asked to leave the room after telling their dog not to eat a treat. In some cases the dog ate it; in others, the researcher removed it. When the owners returned, they were sometimes told, falsely, that the dog had eaten the treat. The dogs showed the so-called guilty look, lowering their heads and avoiding eye contact, most strongly when owners scolded them, whether or not they had actually eaten the treat. The study suggests that the guilty look is a response to the owner's behaviour rather than evidence that the dog feels guilt about its actions. This does not mean dogs lack emotions, but it warns against assuming that animals interpret situations exactly as humans do.",
     "A 2009 study by Alexandra Horowitz tested whether dogs look guilty after misbehaving. Dogs showed the guilty look most strongly when scolded by their owners, whether or not they had eaten a forbidden treat. This suggests the look is a response to the owner's behaviour rather than a sign of guilt, warning against assuming animals interpret situations as humans do.",
     ["guilty look", "scolded", "owner's behaviour", "treat"]),
    ("Under a polar bear's fur",
     "Polar bears look white, but their fur is not actually white. Each hair is transparent and hollow, and it appears white because it scatters visible light, in the same way that snow and ice do. Underneath the thick fur, the bear's skin is black, which may help it absorb heat from sunlight. A thick layer of fat provides further insulation, and polar bears are so well insulated that they can overheat when running. The idea that their hollow hairs channel sunlight down to the skin, like optical fibres, was once popular, but research has not supported it. Polar bears depend on sea ice to hunt seals, their main food, waiting at breathing holes in the ice. As Arctic sea ice forms later in autumn and melts earlier in spring, many bears must spend longer periods on land with little food, which is why they have become a symbol of the effects of climate change.",
     "Polar bear hairs are transparent and hollow, appearing white by scattering light, while the skin underneath is black and thick fat insulates the bears so well that they can overheat. The idea that hairs channel sunlight is unsupported. Bears need sea ice to hunt seals, and as the ice season shortens they spend longer on land with little food.",
     ["transparent", "black", "sea ice", "seals"]),
    ("Transparent frogs",
     "Glass frogs, found in the forests of Central and South America, have skin on their undersides so transparent that their hearts and other organs can be seen. When they rest on leaves during the day, their bodies look pale green and their outline is hard to see, which helps them avoid predators. There was a puzzle, however. Blood is red and should make the frogs easy to spot. In 2022, researchers used special imaging to study sleeping glass frogs and discovered that the animals remove most of the red blood cells from circulation and store them in their liver, which is covered by a reflective layer. This made the frogs two to three times more transparent while resting. When the frogs woke and became active, the red blood cells returned to circulation. Remarkably, the frogs do not seem to suffer from blood clots, and researchers hope to learn from this for medicine.",
     "Glass frogs have transparent undersides that help them hide on leaves. Red blood should make them visible, but a 2022 study found that sleeping frogs store most red blood cells in their reflective liver, becoming much more transparent, and release them on waking. They avoid blood clots while doing this, which researchers hope may help medical research.",
     ["glass frogs", "transparent", "liver", "blood clots"]),
    ("Hoverflies in disguise",
     "Many hoverflies have yellow and black stripes that make them look like wasps or bees, yet they cannot sting. This is an example of Batesian mimicry, named after the nineteenth-century naturalist Henry Walter Bates, who studied butterflies in the Amazon. A harmless species benefits by resembling a dangerous or unpleasant one, because predators that have learned to avoid the dangerous species will also avoid the mimic. The strategy works best when mimics are less common than the species they copy. If mimics become too common, predators may eat many of them without being stung, learn that the pattern is not always dangerous, and begin attacking both. Some hoverflies go beyond appearance, moving their legs in front of their heads to imitate a wasp's long antennae, or buzzing in a similar way. Hoverflies are also important pollinators, and the larvae of some species eat aphids, making them useful in gardens.",
     "Harmless hoverflies resemble stinging wasps, an example of Batesian mimicry, which protects mimics because predators avoid the dangerous model. It works best when mimics are rarer than the models; otherwise predators learn the pattern is harmless. Some hoverflies also copy wasp behaviour. Hoverflies are useful pollinators, and some larvae eat aphids, which helps gardeners.",
     ["Batesian mimicry", "predators", "rarer", "pollinators"]),
    ("Webb's sunshield",
     "The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in December 2021, observes the universe mainly in infrared light, which is emitted by warm objects. This allows it to see very distant galaxies, whose light has been stretched into the infrared, and to look through clouds of dust where stars are forming. But it creates a challenge: the telescope itself must be extremely cold, or its own heat would overwhelm the faint signals it is trying to detect. The solution is a sunshield about the size of a tennis court, made of five thin layers, which blocks heat from the Sun, Earth and Moon. The cold side of the telescope stays at around minus two hundred and thirty degrees Celsius. The telescope also orbits a point in space about one and a half million kilometres from Earth, where it can keep the Sun, Earth and Moon on the same side, behind its shield.",
     "The James Webb Space Telescope observes mainly in infrared, letting it see very distant galaxies and through dust where stars form. Because its own heat would drown faint signals, a five-layer sunshield the size of a tennis court keeps it extremely cold, and it orbits far from Earth, keeping the Sun, Earth and Moon behind the shield.",
     ["infrared", "sunshield", "cold", "distant galaxies"]),
    ("Craters on the Moon",
     "Look at the Moon through binoculars and you will see countless craters, the scars of impacts by asteroids and comets over billions of years. The Earth has been struck just as often, and in fact more, because it is larger and its gravity is stronger. So why does the Earth have so few visible craters? The answer is that our planet constantly renews its surface. Wind, water and ice erode craters over time, plants cover them, and sediments bury them. Over longer periods, the movement of tectonic plates recycles large parts of the Earth's crust. The Earth's atmosphere also burns up many smaller objects before they reach the ground. The Moon has almost no atmosphere, no weather and no moving plates, so its craters can survive almost unchanged for billions of years. This makes the Moon a valuable record of the history of impacts in our part of the solar system.",
     "The Earth has been hit by asteroids and comets at least as often as the Moon, but its surface is constantly renewed by erosion, plants, sediments and moving tectonic plates, and its atmosphere burns up smaller objects. The Moon lacks an atmosphere, weather and moving plates, so its craters survive, preserving a long record of impacts.",
     ["craters", "erosion", "tectonic plates", "atmosphere"]),
    ("Musical chills",
     "Many people experience a shiver or chills when listening to a favourite piece of music. In 2011, researchers in Canada used brain imaging to study this experience. Participants brought music that reliably gave them chills. The researchers found that the brain released dopamine, a chemical associated with pleasure and reward, at the peak moments. Interestingly, dopamine was also released in the moments before the peak, as listeners anticipated what was coming. This suggests that part of the pleasure of music comes from expectation: a skilled composer creates tension and then either satisfies or surprises the listener's predictions. Not everyone experiences musical chills, and studies suggest that people who do may differ in personality, tending to score higher on openness to experience. Some people even have a condition in which music gives them little or no pleasure, although they can still enjoy other rewards such as food.",
     "A 2011 Canadian study found that the brain releases dopamine, linked to pleasure and reward, at the peak moments of music that gives listeners chills, and also just before, during anticipation. This suggests musical pleasure depends partly on expectation. Not everyone experiences chills, and some people gain little pleasure from music while still enjoying other rewards.",
     ["chills", "dopamine", "anticipation", "expectation"]),
    ("Frame rates in cinema",
     "Why are most films shown at twenty-four frames per second? In the silent era, films were shot at a range of speeds, often by cranking the camera by hand. When sound arrived in the late 1920s, the soundtrack was recorded on the same strip of film as the images, so the speed had to be fixed and consistent. Twenty-four frames per second was adopted as a compromise. It was fast enough to produce smooth motion and acceptable sound quality, but slow enough to keep the cost of film, which was expensive, under control. The standard has lasted for nearly a century, even though digital cameras could easily record faster. Some directors have experimented with higher frame rates, which make motion sharper, but many viewers found the result looked too much like television or video, and the slight blur of twenty-four frames has become part of what audiences think a film should look like.",
     "Silent films were shot at varying speeds, but sound required a fixed speed because the soundtrack was recorded on the film. Twenty-four frames per second was a compromise between smooth motion, sound quality and film costs. Although digital cameras can record faster, higher frame rates often looked like television to viewers, so the standard has survived.",
     ["frames per second", "sound", "compromise", "television"]),
    ("Singers behind the screen",
     "In most Hindi films, the actors do not actually sing the songs they appear to perform. Instead, the songs are recorded by professional playback singers, and the actors mime to the recordings on screen. The practice became standard in the 1940s and 1950s, as studios realised that good actors were not always good singers. Some playback singers became among the most famous performers in India. Lata Mangeshkar, for example, recorded thousands of songs over a career lasting more than seven decades and was known across the country, even though audiences rarely saw her on screen. Songs are often released before the film, and their popularity can help a film succeed. Audiences are fully aware of the practice, and a successful partnership between a particular actor and a particular singer's voice could become part of a star's identity. In recent years, some actors have begun singing their own songs, but playback remains common.",
     "In most Hindi films, professional playback singers record the songs while actors mime on screen, a practice standard since the 1940s and 1950s because good actors were not always good singers. Singers such as Lata Mangeshkar became hugely famous. Songs released early can help films succeed, and although some actors now sing, playback remains common.",
     ["playback singers", "mime", "Lata Mangeshkar", "songs"]),
    ("Timbuktu's manuscripts",
     "Timbuktu, in present-day Mali, was a major centre of trade and Islamic scholarship between the fourteenth and sixteenth centuries. Caravans crossing the Sahara brought gold, salt and books, and wealthy families built up private libraries of handwritten manuscripts on subjects including astronomy, medicine, law and mathematics. Many of these manuscripts survived for centuries, kept in family collections, often in difficult conditions. In 2012, armed groups occupied the city, and there were fears that the manuscripts would be destroyed. Librarians and families secretly packed hundreds of thousands of documents into metal trunks and smuggled them south to the capital, Bamako, by road and by river. Some manuscripts were burned, but the great majority were saved. Today scholars are working to preserve and digitise them, and they are helping to challenge the old view that Africa south of the Sahara had little written history before colonial times.",
     "Timbuktu in Mali was a centre of trade and scholarship, and families kept private libraries of manuscripts on astronomy, medicine, law and mathematics. When armed groups occupied the city in 2012, librarians smuggled hundreds of thousands of manuscripts to Bamako, saving most of them. Scholars now preserve and digitise them, challenging the view that the region lacked written history.",
     ["Timbuktu", "manuscripts", "Bamako", "written history"]),
]

SST_ITEMS = []
for idx, (title, transcript, model, keywords) in enumerate(SST_RAW, 1):
    SST_ITEMS.append({
        "id": f"q-pl90-sst-{idx:02d}",
        "type_id": "summarize-spoken-text",
        "type_name": "Summarize Spoken Text",
        "title": title,
        "prompt": "You will hear a short lecture. Write a summary for a fellow student who was not present at the lecture. You should write 50-70 words. You have 10 minutes to finish this task. Your response will be judged on the quality of your writing and on how well your response presents the key points presented in the lecture.",
        "audio_transcript": transcript,
        "prep_time_seconds": 7,
        "time_limit_seconds": 600,
        "options": None,
        "correct_answers": keywords,
        "blanks": None,
        "model_answer": model,
        "explanation": "Key points to include: " + ", ".join(keywords) + ". Model summary should be 50-70 words.",
        "points": 10,
    })
